# skill: insertion_sort

## Contract

Insertion sort implementation — O(n²) time, O(1) space, stable. Efficient for small arrays and nearly-sorted data. Sorting is performed in-place using comparison-based element shifting.

## Dependencies

- `array-base` — array subscript and length conventions
- `comparison` — `compare_gt(a, b)` returns true if a > b

## Slots

| Slot | Type | Description |
|------|------|-------------|
| `arr[]` | `int[]` | Input array to be sorted in-place |
| `n` | `int` | Number of elements in arr |
| `key` | `int` | Temporary variable for current element being inserted |
| `j` | `int` | Index variable for backward traversal |

## Boundaries

- Empty array (`n <= 0`): loop body never executes, returns unchanged
- Single element (`n == 1`): loop body executes once, no shift needed
- Already sorted: inner while loop exits immediately, minimal operations
- Reverse sorted: inner while runs full `i` iterations per outer step, worst case O(n²)
- All equal elements: stable, no swaps beyond initial comparison

## Examples

```c
// Basic usage
int arr[] = {5, 2, 4, 6, 1, 3};
insertion_sort(arr, 6);
// Result: {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}

// Nearly sorted
int arr2[] = {1, 2, 4, 3, 5};
insertion_sort(arr2, 5);
// Result: {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}

// Empty array
int arr3[] = {};
insertion_sort(arr3, 0);
// No-op, safe
```

## Prohibitions

- Memory allocation functions: `malloc`, `calloc`, `realloc`, `free`

---

[code]
// Insertion sort — O(n²), stable, efficient for small/nearly-sorted arrays
void insertion_sort(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        int key = arr[i];
        int j = i - 1;
        while (j >= 0 && compare_gt(arr[j], key)) {
            arr[j + 1] = arr[j];
            j--;
        }
        arr[j + 1] = key;
    }
}
